---
/**
 * /guides/whats-new-in-cot2 - the anchor "here's what's actually
 * different from v83" reference piece.
 *
 * SEO target: 'maplestory classic worlds new content', 'MSCW vs
 * v83 differences', 'maplestory classic worlds what changed',
 * 'MSCW forgotten hollow'. High-intent returning-player query.
 *
 * The framing thesis: v83 GMS is not the spec. MSCW is v83-INSPIRED
 * but Nexon has visibly reshuffled content, added new zones, added
 * new systems (Citizenship, Crafting), and tuned numbers we don't
 * yet know the full extent of. Every guide on the site now cross-
 * links back here so returning players know what to un-remember.
 *
 * Data source: everything called "CoT 2-only" below is derived from
 * cross-referencing the datamine (items.json, maps.json, quests.json)
 * against v83 GMS content lists on Hidden Street / OldSchool wikis.
 * Maps without wzIds are v83-absent by definition (wzId comes from
 * maplestory.io v83 GMS asset db).
 */

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="What's New in Classic World (CoT 2 vs v83) - Liven's Classic Maple Guide"
	description="MapleStory Classic World is not straight-up v83 GMS. Nexon added Forgotten Hollow, the Citizenship system, Crafting, Physical Fitness Test jump quests, plus reworked skills and shifted mob/quest EXP. This is the honest returning-player rundown of what's actually different."
	currentPath="/guides"
	theme="lith"
	decorations={false}
>
	<section class="section-heading">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow eyebrow--guide">CoT 2 vs v83 Reality Check</span>
			<h1>What's New in Classic World</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Every returning player Googles "MapleStory Classic is like v83
				right?" and gets a vibes-based "kinda?" back. Here's the honest
				answer: Nexon added <strong>entire new zones</strong> (Forgotten
				Hollow), <strong>two brand-new systems</strong> (Citizenship,
				Crafting), rewrote at least <strong>one first-job skill list</strong>
				(Bowman), and shifted mob/quest EXP values across the board. The
				v83 wiki is a starting point, not a spec.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose">
		<div class="callout callout--framing">
			<strong>Framing note:</strong> everything below is verified against
			the Closed Online Test 2 client datamine (via ohmi69's
			<a href="https://osmsdataexplorer.com" rel="external nofollow">osmsdataexplorer.com</a>
			and MeowDB's asset dumps). Some numbers may shift again before
			launch. When Founder's Access opens October 6 we'll validate
			live-server behavior and note the diffs on each system's page.
		</div>

		<h2 id="new-zones">New zones that don't exist in v83</h2>
		<p>
			The datamine surfaces 78 named maps that have no v83-GMS wzId
			(the identifier maplestory.io uses to serve v83 assets). These
			are the CoT 2 exclusives, broken down by region:
		</p>

		<h3>Forgotten Hollow (Victoria Island) - 40+ new maps</h3>
		<p>
			The biggest single addition: a whole new region below Victoria
			Island with its own NPC cast (Arthur, Bianca, Raymond, Oak, Flint,
			Roxy, Scarlett, Weston, Silas Irons, Luma, Zelya), its own mob
			composition, and its own quest chains. Includes:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Forgotten Hollow proper</strong>, <strong>Marr's
				Forest</strong>, <strong>Niora Hospital</strong>, and multiple
				"Forgotten Entrance" corridors linking back to Victoria.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Perilous Descent I-III</strong>, <strong>Bygone
				Days</strong>, <strong>The Faded Canopy</strong>,
				<strong>Decayed Tunnel I-III</strong>, and <strong>The Valley of
				Death</strong> - dungeon-style progression maps.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Fully Bloomed Bluebell</strong> and
				<strong>Bluebell Bud</strong> as NPCs (yes, the flowers are
				characters), tied into an early Forgotten Hollow quest line.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Physical Fitness Test - the new event JQ system</h3>
		<p>
			17 CoT 2-only maps make up an entire jump-quest event system with
			difficulty tiers (Easy, Normal, and three numbered Levels).
			Includes a Waiting Room, per-level rooms ("Up and Up", "Coconut
			Season"), and Bianca as the gating NPC. This looks like Nexon's
			take on the classic OMOK-style event rotation, permanent instead
			of seasonal.
		</p>

		<h3>Henesys Town Hall + civic buildings</h3>
		<p>
			The Citizenship system introduced a physical <strong>Town Hall</strong>
			in Henesys (and presumably Kerning City) that houses citizenship
			registration, contribution turn-ins, and shop rotations. This
			building doesn't exist in v83 at all - see the
			<a href="/citizenship">Citizenship guide</a> for how the whole
			system works.
		</p>

		<h3>Other structural additions</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Guild Head Quarters &lt;Hall of Fame&gt;</strong> in
				Orbis - dedicated guild-showcase map, brand new.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Orbis Ticketing Booth</strong> - reworked ticketing
				interface for the ship travel network.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Reworked Maple Island entry maps</strong> - "Mushroom
				Town - West Entrance", "East Entrance to Mushroom Town", "A
				Split Road", "Snail Hunting Ground I" all show up as CoT 2-only
				renames/expansions of the tutorial flow.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Free Market instances 1-N</strong> - the FM channel
				splits work differently in CoT 2 (details TBD until we're on
				live servers to see the actual queue behavior).
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="new-systems">Two brand-new systems layered on top</h2>

		<h3>Citizenship (Henesys or Kerning City)</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Not a v83 system at all.</strong> At Lv 12, you pick a
			town allegiance, earn contribution via daily quests and events,
			and climb 10 grades to unlock town-exclusive gear, chairs, and
			cosmetic access. Kicks off the region-locked shop meta and
			introduces "resident vs traveler" NPC dialogue paths.
		</p>
		<p>
			Full breakdown in <a href="/citizenship">the Citizenship guide</a> -
			cover story quests, grade thresholds, and switching-cost math.
		</p>

		<h3>Crafting</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Also not a v83 system.</strong> Weaponcrafting +
			Woodcrafting kits let you assemble gear from datamined ETC drops
			and materials. Ties into the "never sell ETC" beginner advice
			hard - if you sold your Leaves at Lv 12, you can't craft the Lv
			15 gloves at Lv 18.
		</p>
		<p>
			Full breakdown in <a href="/crafting">the Crafting guide</a> -
			all recipes, material sources, and cost math.
		</p>

		<h2 id="skill-reworks">First-job skill changes</h2>
		<p>
			CoT 2 keeps most v83 first-job skill kits intact but there are
			confirmed changes on two classes. Full details in
			<a href="/guides/skill-changes">the Skill Changes guide</a>.
			Summary:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Warrior:</strong> gained a new 6th passive skill
				<em>Precise Strikes</em> (accuracy + crit rate). Other 5
				skills unchanged.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Bowman:</strong> lost <em>Blessing of Amazon</em>
				(the classic v83 party attack buff), gained <em>Power
				Knockback</em> (crowd-control AoE that always crits).
				Significant build re-think for returners.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Magician + Thief:</strong> all six first-job skills
				match the v83 baseline exactly. No changes. (Datamine calls
				Thief "Rogue" internally, but the visible class name is
				unchanged.)
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="exp-shifts">EXP curve shifts</h2>
		<p>
			Both mob EXP and quest EXP values in the datamine differ from
			commonly-cited v83 numbers, though the shifts are inconsistent
			(some go up, some down, some stay the same). This is the least
			settled area right now - we know the numbers are different but we
			don't have a confirmed pattern.
		</p>
		<p>
			Full analysis in <a href="/guides/exp-then-vs-now">Then vs Now:
			EXP & Progression</a> - includes concrete mob EXP examples
			pulled directly from the datamine.
		</p>

		<h2 id="what-hasnt-changed">What HASN'T changed (that you can still trust)</h2>
		<p>
			Not everything in v83 muscle memory is worth un-learning. Verified
			<strong>identical to v83</strong> in the CoT 2 datamine:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Class advancement flow:</strong> Beginner &rarr; job
				at 8/10, 2nd job at 30, 3rd job at 70, 4th job at 120. Same
				NPCs (Dances with Balrog, Grendel, Athena Pierce, Dark Lord)
				in the same towns.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Base stat mechanics:</strong> STR/DEX/INT/LUK all
				work the same. HP/MP AP allocation, HP/MP washing mechanics,
				equipment stat scaling all unchanged.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Item categories + IDs:</strong> Every v83 item ID
				that carried over kept its ID. Red Potion is still 2000000.
				Chaos Scroll is still Chaos Scroll. Set bonuses still exist.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Quest flow archetype:</strong> Talk-to-NPC &rarr;
				collect-N-of-X &rarr; return-for-EXP-and-mesos. Structure
				identical; the specific quest numbers just shifted.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Magician + Thief first-job kits:</strong> as noted
				above, no changes for these two classes.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="what-we-dont-yet-know">What we don't yet know</h2>
		<p>
			Honest gaps in current knowledge (all should resolve during
			Founder's Access starting October 6):
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Exact party EXP formula.</strong> The v83 formula was
				<code>0.6/partySize + 0.4*(memberLv/totalLv)</code> - our
				<a href="/guides/party-exp">Party EXP guide</a> uses it as a
				working assumption, but we haven't confirmed CoT 2 uses the
				same math. Nexon may have retuned the party bonus curve.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Which classic PQs actually ship at launch.</strong>
				Kerning PQ is confirmed. Ludibrium PQ, Amoria PQ, Ludi Maze,
				Orbis PQ, Herb Town PQ, Guild PQ - all TBD until we're on
				live servers to check the NPC roster.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>2nd + 3rd job skill kits.</strong> Datamine has the
				skill files but we've only surfaced the 1st-job changes so
				far. 2nd job class-splits (Fighter/Page/Spearman etc.) and
				beyond will need a second pass.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>End-game boss lists.</strong> Zakum, Horntail, Pink
				Bean - which are in at launch, which have been retuned, and
				which are new/different. Total blank slate right now.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Meso drop rates + mob EXP live-server values.</strong>
				Datamine gives us <em>declared</em> values but Nexon's live
				tuning multipliers (event bonuses, base rate changes) aren't
				in the client files.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="reading-order">Suggested reading order for returners</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>
				This page, first - get the "what's actually different"
				overview under your belt.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/guides/skill-changes">Skill Changes Per Class</a> -
				before you commit to a class based on v83 memory, check what
				changed in the kit.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/guides/exp-then-vs-now">Then vs Now: EXP &
				Progression</a> - understand which of your grinding-spot
				memories still apply.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/citizenship">Citizenship</a> and
				<a href="/crafting">Crafting</a> - the two new systems that
				have zero v83 analog and gate serious progression.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/guides/getting-started">Getting Started</a> - our
				beginner-flow walkthrough, adjusted for CoT 2 realities.
			</li>
		</ol>

		<p class="closing">
			This page is a living document. As Founder's Access reveals
			live-server tuning that differs from the CoT 2 datamine, the
			deltas get logged here first, then propagated to the affected
			guides. Bookmark it if you're returning from an old GMS
			character - it'll save you a class-rerolling reset.
		</p>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.section-heading {
		max-width: 68ch;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
	}

	.prose {
		max-width: 72ch;
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.62;
		color: var(--text-body);
	}
	.prose h2 {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose h3 {
		margin-top: 1.4rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.15rem;
	}
	.prose p,
	.prose ul,
	.prose ol {
		margin-top: 0.7rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
	}
	.prose li {
		margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose code {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.88em;
		background: var(--bg-cloud);
		padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
		border-radius: 0.25rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
	}
	.prose a {
		color: var(--accent-primary);
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-thickness: 0.09em;
		text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
	}
	.prose strong {
		color: var(--text-heading);
	}
	.prose .closing {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
